; 22-prove-a-law-of-logic.kurt
;
; Prove a law of logic
;
; Contraposition, (A ⇒ B) ≡ (¬B ⇒ ¬A), from the rules of prop.kurt alone. First a lemma,
; (A ⇒ B) ≡ (¬A ∨ B): an equivalence is proven as two implications (`iff-intro`), each in a
; `show` / `proof` / `qed` of its own. Inside, the lessons so far come together: an implication by
; `assume` (lesson 02), cases (lesson 04), a contradiction (lesson 03). The cases of the first
; implication are A and ¬A: the disjunction A ∨ ¬A is the excluded middle, which prop.kurt proves
; and exports as "excluded-middle", so Kurt prints the last step `by or-elim(excluded-middle, ...)`.
; Then the theorem is a chain of equivalences (below).
;
; `var A, B` makes the two propositions variables: the theorem holds for every A and B, and
; another file that loads this one can use it with any formulas for them.

load prop
var A, B

; lemma
show A ⇒ B  ≡  ¬A ∨ B
proof
    show (A ⇒ B)  ⇒  (¬A ∨ B)
    proof
        assume A ⇒ B
            assume A
                B
                ¬A ∨ B
            assume ¬A
                ¬A ∨ B
            ¬A ∨ B                ; by or-elim, on A ∨ ¬A
    qed
    show (¬A ∨ B)  ⇒  (A ⇒ B)
    proof
        assume ¬A ∨ B
            assume A
                assume ¬A
                    contradiction
                    B
                assume B
                    B
                B
    qed
    A ⇒ B  ≡  ¬A ∨ B
qed

; theorem
show (A ⇒ B)  ≡  (¬B ⇒ ¬A)   "contraposition"
proof
    A ⇒ B  ≡  ¬A ∨ B
           ≡  B ∨ ¬A
           ≡  ¬¬B ∨ ¬A
           ≡  ¬B ⇒ ¬A
qed

; The lines of the chain are justified differently. Line 50 is line 49 again for Kurt (`∨` is
; symmetric, so `¬A ∨ B` and `B ∨ ¬A` are the same formula), hence `by 49`. Line 51 replaces B
; by ¬¬B with prop's "not-not" (`iff-subst(not-not, 49)`). Line 52 joins line 51 with the lemma
; of line 49, taken for ¬B and ¬A (`chain-trans-0-0(51, 49)`, the transitivity of `≡`).
;
; The same proofs, and the other laws of the natural deduction (double negation, de Morgan, the
; excluded middle on its own), are in proofs/natural-deduction/.
